  "promoCardLabel": "OFFER FROM THIS APP",
  "@promoCardLabel": {
    "description": "The visible promotional label on the in-app upgrade card. ONE label plus a distinct container is what satisfies Apple 2.5.18, Microsoft Store 10.10.4, Play's native-ads trigger and India's Disguised Advertisement pattern at once — a promotional surface may never be styled as app content. It is deliberately not the word 'Ad': this card promotes THIS app, which matches none of Play's three ads-declaration triggers (research/44 V2), so the honest word is 'offer'. Overridable per app via AppConfig.copy['promo.card.label']."
  },
  "promoCardTitle": "Get the full experience",
  "@promoCardTitle": {
    "description": "Headline of the same-app upgrade card. Overridable via AppConfig.copy['promo.card.title'] (and ['promo.card.title.b'] for the B variant), so a campaign changes wording with no release."
  },
  "promoCardBody": "Everything unlocked, on every device you sign in on.",
  "@promoCardBody": {
    "description": "Body of the same-app upgrade card. States a benefit and makes NO claim about urgency, scarcity or a discount: false urgency is unfair in all circumstances under UCPD Annex I(7) and is named by India's CCPA Dark Patterns Guidelines 2023. Overridable via AppConfig.copy['promo.card.body']."
  },
  "promoCardPrice": "{price}, billed per {term}",
  "@promoCardPrice": {
    "description": "🔴 AN ABSOLUTE PRICE AND NOTHING ELSE. {price} is DERIVED from the rail's own amount and currency (Offering.formattedPrice) — never typed — and there is no 'was', no percentage and no 'save X'. Directive 98/6/EC Art 6a and CJEU C-330/23 require any announced reduction to be computed against the lowest price of the prior 30 days, and this repository holds no price history, so the comparison cannot be made and must not be shown.",
    "placeholders": {
      "price": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "$4.99"
      },
      "term": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "month"
      }
    }
  },
  "promoCardDismissA11y": "Dismiss this offer",
  "@promoCardDismissA11y": {
    "description": "What a screen reader announces for the card's icon-only close control. An icon-only control with no accessible name is a dismissal a user cannot perform, which turns a dismissible card into an undismissable one for exactly the people least able to work around it."
  },
  "legalAcceptTerms": "I am 18 or older, I agree to the Terms of Service, and I have read the Privacy Policy.",
  "@legalAcceptTerms": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/legal_consent_fields.dart. The BLOCKING sign-up clickwrap (research/43), and since 2026-09-05 the 18+ ATTESTATION required by [ADR 068] as well - the tick is the affirmative act, so the age floor rides on the control that already blocks registration rather than on a line nobody has to touch. ONE COMPLETE SENTENCE, with the two document links rendered as separate controls beneath it rather than as spans inside it — a rich-text 'I agree to the [Terms] and [Privacy Policy]' fixes English word order and leaves a translator splicing links into the middle of a clause whose verb belongs at the end. The wording distinguishes AGREEING to the terms from having READ the notice, which is the legal difference between the two documents."
  },
  "legalMarketingOptIn": "Email me occasional product news and offers. You can unsubscribe at any time.",
  "@legalMarketingOptIn": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/legal_consent_fields.dart. The EXPRESS, OPTIONAL, DEFAULT-OFF marketing opt-in (research/44 rider, ADR 040). Must never be pre-ticked and must never block sign-up. Names the unsubscribe route in the same sentence because an opt-in that does not say how to leave is not an informed one."
  },
  "legalMustAcceptTerms": "Please accept the Terms of Service to create an account.",
  "@legalMustAcceptTerms": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/login_screen.dart. Shown when the sign-up arm is submitted from the KEYBOARD (onSubmitted) with the terms box untouched — the button is already disabled, so this covers the path that does not go through the button."
  },
  "verifyEmailTitle": "Confirm your email",
  "@verifyEmailTitle": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/verify_email_screen.dart. Title of the only screen an unverified session can reach."
  },
  "verifyEmailBody": "We sent a confirmation link to {email}. Open it to finish setting up your account.",
  "@verifyEmailBody": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/verify_email_screen.dart. States the address the link went to, so a mistyped one is visible here rather than discovered by an email that never arrives.",
    "placeholders": {
      "email": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "alex@example.com"
      }
    }
  },
  "verifyEmailSpamHint": "No email yet? Check your spam or junk folder.",
  "@verifyEmailSpamHint": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/verify_email_screen.dart."
  },
  "verifyEmailContinue": "I have confirmed my email",
  "@verifyEmailContinue": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/verify_email_screen.dart. Re-reads the user from the identity provider. The control exists because confirmation happens in a MAIL CLIENT on a link the app never sees, so nothing pushes the new state at a running app."
  },
  "verifyEmailResend": "Send the email again",
  "@verifyEmailResend": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/verify_email_screen.dart. Resends to the address on the CURRENT session — the screen cannot aim it anywhere else."
  },
  "verifyEmailResent": "Sent. Have another look in your inbox.",
  "@verifyEmailResent": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/verify_email_screen.dart. Shown inline rather than as a SnackBar: a successful confirmation REPLACES this page via the router gate, and a SnackBar on a page being torn down is a message nobody reads."
  },
  "verifyEmailStillUnverified": "Not confirmed yet. Open the link in the email, then try again.",
  "@verifyEmailStillUnverified": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/verify_email_screen.dart. Still-unverified is a real answer, not an error — the router leaves the user here and this says why."
  },
  "checkInboxTitle": "Check your inbox",
  "@checkInboxTitle": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/check_inbox_screen.dart. The destination for a sign-up that returned a user and NO SESSION (Supabase 'Confirm email' ON). Distinct from verifyEmailTitle, which serves a session whose address is unconfirmed — this person is signed OUT, so the router's sessionIsUnverified gate never fires for them."
  },
  "checkInboxBody": "We sent a confirmation link to {email}. Open it to activate your account.",
  "@checkInboxBody": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/check_inbox_screen.dart. Names the address so a mistyped one is visible here — it is the only screen in this flow that can show it, because there is no session to read it back from.",
    "placeholders": {
      "email": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "you@example.com"
      }
    }
  },
  "checkInboxThenSignIn": "Once you have opened the link, come back and sign in. Your account is not active until then.",
  "@checkInboxThenSignIn": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/check_inbox_screen.dart. States the SECOND step: confirming happens in a mail client, often on another device, so the app cannot carry the user through it and must say what to do afterwards."
  },
  "checkInboxBackToSignIn": "Back to sign in",
  "@checkInboxBackToSignIn": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/check_inbox_screen.dart. The primary action, because signing in is the next thing this app can do for them. Without it the screen is a dead end, which is the defect it exists to remove."
  },
  "reacceptTermsTitle": "Our terms have changed",
  "@reacceptTermsTitle": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reaccept_terms_screen.dart. The MATERIAL-CHANGE re-acceptance interstitial (research/43), shown when kTermsVersion or kPrivacyPolicyVersion has moved past what the user accepted."
  },
  "reacceptTermsBody": "We have updated our Terms of Service and Privacy Policy. Please review them and accept to carry on.",
  "@reacceptTermsBody": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reaccept_terms_screen.dart."
  },
  "reacceptTermsAccept": "Accept and continue",
  "@reacceptTermsAccept": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reaccept_terms_screen.dart. Disabled until the (unticked) box is ticked — the tick is the affirmative act, and a button that works without it makes the box decorative."
  },
  "resetPasswordTitle": "Set a new password",
  "@resetPasswordTitle": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reset_password_screen.dart. The screen a password-reset link lands on. `sendPasswordReset` shipped without this screen existing at all, so the mail was delivered and the link had nowhere to go."
  },
  "resetPasswordBody": "Choose a new password for your account. You will sign in with it from now on.",
  "@resetPasswordBody": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reset_password_screen.dart. States the consequence rather than the mechanism — the user asked to get back in, not to perform an update."
  },
  "resetPasswordNew": "New password",
  "@resetPasswordNew": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reset_password_screen.dart. Field label."
  },
  "resetPasswordConfirm": "New password again",
  "@resetPasswordConfirm": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reset_password_screen.dart. The confirmation field. NOT 'Confirm password' — that reads as a button on a screen whose real button is elsewhere, and this is the one field whose purpose has to survive translation intact."
  },
  "resetPasswordSubmit": "Save new password",
  "@resetPasswordSubmit": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reset_password_screen.dart."
  },
  "resetPasswordEnterOne": "Enter a new password.",
  "@resetPasswordEnterOne": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reset_password_screen.dart for NewPasswordProblem.empty. Distinct from passwordTooShort because an untouched field needs 'fill this in', not 'try harder'."
  },
  "resetPasswordMismatch": "The two passwords do not match.",
  "@resetPasswordMismatch": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reset_password_screen.dart for NewPasswordProblem.mismatched. 🔴 THE ONE ERROR THE SERVER CANNOT CATCH: both boxes hold well-formed passwords, so gotrue would accept the wrong one and lock the user out of the account they just recovered."
  },
  "resetPasswordDone": "Your password is set. Sign in with it now.",
  "@resetPasswordDone": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reset_password_screen.dart. The terminal state, shown INLINE and left standing until the user taps sign-in — the sign-out that ends the flow would otherwise tear this page down before the sentence could be read ([ADR 027])."
  },
  "resetPasswordLinkDead": "This reset link cannot be used here.",
  "@resetPasswordLinkDead": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reset_password_screen.dart when the screen is reached with no recovery session — an expired link, one already used, or one opened where the PKCE code verifier was never stored. Says nothing about whether an account exists."
  },
  "resetPasswordLinkExpiredHint": "Reset links stop working after a while, and each one can only be used once. Ask for a new one from the sign-in screen.",
  "@resetPasswordLinkExpiredHint": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reset_password_screen.dart when the arrival is AuthLinkProblem.expiredOrUsed — gotrue answered the link with error_code=otp_expired. Kept SEPARATE from resetPasswordSameDeviceHint because that one tells the user to open the link on the device they asked from, which is useless advice to somebody who did exactly that and simply waited too long. Says nothing about whether an account exists."
  },
  "resetPasswordSameDeviceHint": "Open the link on the same device and browser you asked for it from, or ask for a new one from the sign-in screen.",
  "@resetPasswordSameDeviceHint": {
    "description": "Consumed by features/auth/reset_password_screen.dart. 🔴 A PROPERTY OF PKCE, NOT A BUG: the code verifier is generated by and kept in the installation that REQUESTED the link, so a link opened elsewhere arrives with nothing to exchange. This sentence is the only thing that turns a dead end into an action."
  },
  "promotionalOffers": "Offers and promotions",
  "@promotionalOffers": {
    "description": "Heading for the GDPR Art 21 objection row. 🔴 THE VALUE IS THE PAGE'S WORDS, NOT THE OTHER WAY ROUND. sites/nikatru/privacy.html §3 (owner-signed 2026-08-10) tells the reader \"Open the app's Settings and turn Offers and promotions off\", and r4 had shipped this string as \"Promotional offers\" — a person following the published instruction would look for a heading that was not there. The page is the promise and is [ADR 031] class B owner copy; an app label is class A, so the app moved. Changed in apps/subscriptiontracker and in the brick, en and ta together. Named for what it governs (offers), not for the mechanism (consent), because the user is looking for the thing they read about."
  },
  "promoObjectionExplain": "You can stop seeing offers for our apps at any time. Nothing else changes.",
  "@promoObjectionExplain": {
    "description": "Art 21(4) requires the right to object to be presented clearly and separately. The second sentence is load-bearing: it says objecting costs nothing, so the control is not read as a trade."
  },
  "promoStopOffers": "Stop showing offers",
  "@promoStopOffers": {
    "description": "The objection action. NEVER confirm-shaming ('No thanks, I don't want to save money') — India's CCPA Dark Patterns Guidelines 2023 forbid it and research/44 V5 lists it as forbidden in all configurations."
  },
  "promoResumeOffers": "Show offers again",
  "@promoResumeOffers": {
    "description": "The way back. Art 21 gives a right to object, not a duty to stay objected, so a one-way control would be a trap."
  },
  "promoOffersOff": "Offers are off",
  "@promoOffersOff": {
    "description": "The objected state, stated as an effect. A control whose state the user cannot see is a button that appears to do nothing."
  },
  "promoLabel": "Promotion",
  "@promoLabel": {
    "description": "THE ONE STRING THE STORES ACTUALLY MANDATE. It is the visible label on PromoSurface, and one word satisfies Apple 2.5.18 ('must clearly indicate that they are an ad'), Microsoft 10.10.4 ('clearly distinguishable from other content'), Play's native-ads trigger and India's Disguised Advertisement dark pattern at once. It exists here with no consumer yet BECAUSE the first promotional card is the caller: PromoSurface's English default would otherwise ship untranslated, and a Tamil user would meet the one word a reviewer checks for in English."
  },
